The bug hunt result is written and validated. Here is my final verdict.

## Summary

I validated the §2.3 Distributed-PRD include-dedup feature (`P1.M1.T1/T2`) in `src/core/session-utils.ts`.

**What works correctly (verified):**
- All 43 existing unit tests pass (`prd-resolve.test.ts`, `prd-markers.test.ts`)
- Core global-flat-dedup + elision is correct: shared-set-by-reference dedup works for diamonds, mutual/self-cycles, and entry back-edges (each file imported exactly once); document-order first-encounter is preserved; elision is never verbatim; the base invariant (project-root-relative) holds across nested sub-dirs; boundary/existence rules match §2.3; stale-`.md` warnings go to stderr
- **The project's own real distributed PRD** (`spec/SPEC.md` → 16 `@`-included spec files, 157 KB) resolves with **0 surviving `@spec/` tokens** and is **byte-idempotent with a stable hash**
- ~20 adversarial probes covered: marker-on diamond idempotency, file-named-include collision, deep chains, unicode paths, CRLF, dir-vs-file prefix collisions, document-order reordering, empty-file dedup, shared-file-first ordering, case-sensitive `.MD`, maxDepth env edge values, trailing-slash dirs

**Two minor edge-case deviations found** (both real spec deviations, both with contrived/pathological triggers — the core feature is ship-ready for realistic PRDs):
1. **BUG-001** — Marker-mode idempotency breaks if a file named like a marker word (`include`/`end-include`/`include-ref`) exists; the markers contain `@tokens` of their own, violating §2.3's marker requirement. Marker mode is default-off and the trigger is contrived.
2. **BUG-002** — The unconditional idempotency MUST breaks for include chains deeper than `PRD_INCLUDE_MAX_DEPTH`; depth-exceeded resolvable tokens survive neither expanded nor elided, so they expand on the second pass. Pathological under default 10, more reachable if a user lowers the limit.

I wrote only to the required path; no `TEST_RESULTS.md`/source/`plan/` modifications.

